@page "/learn/tempdata-ssr"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>TempData · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">TempData</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">You save an order and redirect back to the list. How does the list know to say "saved"?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Pages &amp; navigation · Static SSR · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> TempData for Blazor components, the one-time message MVC and Razor Pages always
        had. In .NET 10 components had no TempData: a message that had to survive a redirect went in the URL or in a
        cookie you wrote yourself.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is an <i>Orders</i> list. Click an order, write an internal note, save, and you're redirected back to
        the list, which should say "Order … saved" once.
    </p>
    <p>
        This one does it the quick .NET 10 way: the edit page redirects to the list with <code>?saved=…</code> in the
        address. Under the list, <b>Carried by</b> says what brought the message.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The list, with a flag in the URL" Url="demo/tempdata/orders/net10"
                Label=".NET 10: a flag in the URL" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            Start with the link under the list, the kind someone could send you: click
            <code>…/net10?saved=99999</code>. The list says "Order 99999 saved", for an order that doesn't exist. Then
            do it for real: click an order number, type a note and press <b>Save changes</b>. The list says "Order …
            saved", and <b>Carried by</b> shows the address ending <code>?saved=…</code>. Now press the <b>⟳</b>
            refresh button in the frame's address bar (not <b>Start over</b>): it says "saved" again.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>The message is whatever the address says</h2>
    <p>The flag isn't a message from the save. It's part of the page's address:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">What the list sees</span>
        <pre class="snip">POST /orders/11078   <span class="dim">→ 302 Location: /orders?saved=11078</span>
GET  /orders?saved=11078   <span class="ok">✓ "Order 11078 saved"</span>
GET  /orders?saved=11078   <span class="dim">(refresh)</span>   <span class="err">"Order 11078 saved" again</span>
GET  /orders?saved=99999   <span class="dim">(a shared link)</span>   <span class="err">"Order 99999 saved", nothing saved</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The list can't tell a redirect from a refresh, a bookmark or a link someone typed. Anything in the address
        is replayed every time the address is opened.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Set the message as you redirect, read it once</h2>
    <p>
        The edit page writes the message through a <code>[SupplyParameterFromTempData]</code> property. The list
        reads it a different way, with <code>ITempData.Get</code>, and that difference matters: the attribute writes
        its property back to TempData when the request ends, so a list that read through it would keep the message
        and show it again on the next visit. <code>Get</code> reads it once and lets it go.
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">// The edit page</span>
<span class="hl">[SupplyParameterFromTempData(Name = "flash")]</span> public string? Flash { get; set; }

<span class="hl">Flash = $"Order {Id} saved";</span>
Navigation.NavigateTo("orders");

<span class="dim">// The list</span>
<span class="hl">[CascadingParameter] public ITempData? TempData { get; set; }</span>

message = <span class="hl">TempData?.Get("flash")</span> as string;</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The redirect sets a cookie holding the message, encrypted and signed, so an edited cookie is ignored.
        <code>Get</code> reads the value and marks it for deletion, so the list's response deletes the cookie. There's
        nothing to set up in <code>Program.cs</code>: <code>AddRazorComponents()</code> already includes TempData.
        Here's the same list, with TempData.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same list, with TempData" Url="demo/tempdata/orders"
                Label=".NET 11: TempData">
        <Caption>
            Click an order number, type a note and press <b>Save changes</b>. The list says "Order … saved", the
            address has no flag, and <b>Carried by</b> shows an encrypted TempData cookie. Press the <b>⟳</b> refresh
            button in the frame's address bar: the message has gone, and <b>Carried by</b> says "no TempData
            cookie".
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Here are the two pages behind Fig. 2:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/TempData/EditOrder.razor" Feature="tempdata-ssr">
        <Caption>
            The edit page. Where Fig. 1's page put <code>?saved=</code> on the redirect, this one sets
            <code>Flash</code>, and the redirect carries it in the cookie. (Its hand-typed
            <code>label for</code>/<code>id</code> pair predates the guide's Labels page; it isn't part of this
            technique.)
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/TempData/OrderList.razor" Feature="tempdata-ssr">
        <Caption>
            The list. <code>Get</code> reads the message once, which is why Fig. 2's refresh shows nothing.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>A flag in the URL, or a cookie you write yourself</h2>
    <p>A .NET 10 build of both workarounds, driven with curl. The cookie copies the Identity template's own pattern, 5-second lifetime included:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Two ways to say "saved" in .NET 10, and what happened</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">NavigateTo($"orders?saved={Id}");</pre>
                <span class="why">the list shows a banner when <code>?saved=</code> is there</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ a refresh shows it again; <code>?saved=99999</code> shows it with nothing saved</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">Response.Cookies.Append("Status",
    $"Order {Id} saved",
    new() { MaxAge = <span class="err">TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)</span> });</pre>
                <span class="why">the list reads it and deletes it, as the Identity template's <code>IdentityRedirectManager</code> does for <code>StatusMessage</code></span>
                <div class="verdict ok">✓ shows once; but as written it's plain text a forged cookie can replace, and the template's 5-second lifetime drops it on a slow redirect</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">[CascadingParameter] ITempData? TempData
[SupplyParameterFromTempData]</pre>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ CS0246: neither type exists in .NET 10</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        The cookie works. Drop the <code>MaxAge</code> and protect the value with Data Protection, and it's sound.
        But that's plumbing every app wrote and copied by hand. An app that also registered MVC could borrow MVC's
        TempData through <code>ITempDataDictionaryFactory</code> and the <code>HttpContext</code>, but loading and
        saving it was still code you wrote: nothing in Blazor wired it to a component.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>TempData, built in</h2>
    <p>
        That plumbing is now part of Blazor: statically rendered components get the request's
        <code>ITempData</code> as a cascading parameter, an encrypted cookie carries it, and
        <code>[SupplyParameterFromTempData]</code> binds a value to a property. That's what Fig. 2 runs.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>Reading through the attribute?</b> If you must, set the property to null after reading it, or the message is written back and shows again; <code>Get</code>, as above, is simpler.</li>
        <li><b>Simple values only.</b> Strings, ints, bools, Guids, <code>DateTime</code>s and int enums, plus arrays, lists and dictionaries of those. Anything else throws when TempData is saved, even a <code>decimal</code>: an order total of <code>42.50m</code> won't go in as a number. Format it into the message string, or store the order id and look the rest up.</li>
        <li><b>Static SSR pages only.</b> An interactive component has no HTTP request, so <code>ITempData</code> cascades as null there and <code>TempData?.Get</code> gives nothing.</li>
        <li><b>An encrypted cookie, by default.</b> It lasts for the browser session and is deleted by the response that reads it. To keep values in server session instead, set <code>TempDataProviderType.SessionStorage</code> in <code>AddRazorComponents</code> options, and add <code>AddDistributedMemoryCache()</code>, <code>AddSession()</code> and <code>UseSession()</code>.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
